---
/**
 * `/guides/foreign-currency-income` — from `docs/research/05-foreign-currency-service-income.md`
 * and `docs/personas/p1-wfh-foreign-consultant.md`.
 *
 * The research document's own framing: "No consumer calculator asks about
 * the remittance condition. Being the tool that does is the point of this
 * project" — and the reason this page exists is the same reason: everyone
 * searching this question has heard "it's exempt" or "it's taxed", and the
 * true answer is neither, on its own.
 *
 * WHAT IS STATED AS SETTLED VS FLAGGED AS OPEN, AND WHY
 *
 * The regime, the rate and the operative wording are `verified` in the
 * research document. Q10 (timing), Q11 (partial remittance), Q12 (evidence),
 * Q13 (FX account) and Q41 (payment intermediaries) are `unverified` there,
 * and stay that way here — this page states none of them as settled, per
 * CLAUDE.md rule 3 and the prompt's own "do not restate a figure that the
 * research marks unverified as though it were settled".
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AsAtStamp from '../../components/AsAtStamp.astro';
import { Callout, CitationRef } from '../../components/ui/index.ts';
import { loadTaxYears } from '../../lib/tax/load.ts';
import '../../styles/guides.css';

const taxYear = loadTaxYears()[0]!.data;
---

<Base
  title="Is money I earn from overseas clients taxed in Sri Lanka? — Sri Lanka Tax Return Support"
  description="From 1 April 2025 foreign-currency service income is taxable, at a reduced maximum rate — but only if the money is remitted through a bank to Sri Lanka. What that condition means, and what is still unclear about it."
>
  <div class="guide measure">
    <p class="guide__back"><a href="/guides/">All guides</a></p>

    <h1 class="guide__question">
      Is money I earn from overseas clients taxed in Sri Lanka?
    </h1>

    <p class="guide__answer">
      Yes, from 1 April 2025. If you are resident in Sri Lanka and are paid in foreign
      currency by clients outside the country, that income is taxable — but it can qualify
      for a reduced maximum rate of{' '}
      <strong class="figure">15%</strong>{' '}
      instead of the ordinary bands.{' '}
      <CitationRef
        src="act-2-2025#s.3(1)(d) — IRA Sch.1 para 1(6)"
        sources={taxYear.sources}
        label="Rate from"
      />{' '}
      Whether you get the reduced rate depends on exactly how the money reached you, and that
      is the part most people get wrong.
    </p>

    <AsAtStamp
      yearOfAssessment={taxYear.yearOfAssessment}
      dataReviewedOn={taxYear.lastReviewed}
    />

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What changed on 1 April 2025</h2>
      <p>
        Before that date, income from services rendered to overseas clients and other
        foreign-source income was broadly exempt from Sri Lankan income tax. That
        exemption was removed with effect from 1 April 2025.{' '}
        <CitationRef
          src="act-2-2025#s.1(2)"
          sources={taxYear.sources}
          label="Removal from"
        />{' '}
        If you have been filing nothing for the past few years because you correctly understood
        this income to be exempt, that is no longer the position for income earned on or after
        that date.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>The rate is a cap on the normal bands, not a separate table</h2>
      <p>
        The law does not give this income its own flat rate. It takes the ordinary
        progressive bands that apply to everyone's income and caps the rate charged on
        qualifying gains and profits at{' '}
        <strong class="figure">15%</strong>, whatever band they would otherwise fall into.
        {' '}
        <CitationRef
          src="act-2-2025#s.3(1)(d) — IRA Sch.1 para 1(6)"
          sources={taxYear.sources}
        />{' '}
        The bottom band of the ordinary bands, for the income within it, is below that cap and
        is unaffected; every band above the cap is charged at 15% instead. The exact bands for
        the year you are working out — including where the cap starts to bite — are on the <a
          href="/rates/">rates page</a
        >, generated from the same figures the calculator uses.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>The condition that actually decides it</h2>
      <p>The reduced rate applies only where two things are both true:</p>
      <blockquote>
        the payment for such services is received in foreign currency and remitted through
        a bank to Sri Lanka
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        <CitationRef
          src="act-2-2025#s.3(1)(d) — IRA Sch.1 para 1(6)(a)"
          sources={taxYear.sources}
          label="Operative wording from"
        />{' '}
        Being paid in dollars is not enough on its own — the money also has to make it to Sri
        Lanka through a bank. Earnings you keep in an account outside Sri Lanka fall back to
        the ordinary bands — the ones the 15% cap exists to reduce — and there is no lower fallback
        rate for them.
      </p>
      <p>
        One correction worth knowing: the Act says "a bank", not "a <em>licensed</em> bank".
        The licensing qualifier that circulates in secondary summaries is not in the statutory
        text.{' '}
        <CitationRef src="act-2-2025#s.3" sources={taxYear.sources} />
      </p>

      <h3>Where this actually gets uncertain</h3>
      <p>
        Almost nobody's income arrives by one clean route, and the Act does not define
        "remitted" or say what counts as reaching Sri Lanka "through a bank". This project
        has checked the primary sources it holds and found these points genuinely open —
        not just unclear to a layperson, but not settled anywhere this project can point
        to:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A foreign-currency account at a Sri Lankan bank.</strong> Money paid into
          an account you hold at a bank here, but kept in dollars rather than converted to rupees
          — does that count as remitted? Not established.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Wise, Payoneer, PayPal and similar services.</strong> Your client pays into
          a balance with one of those, and you withdraw it to your Sri Lankan bank afterwards
          — does the intermediary break the chain, or does the money still count as remitted
          through a bank once it lands here? Not established.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Part of your earnings remitted, part kept abroad.</strong> This is the most
          common real pattern, and the Act does not say whether the reduced rate then applies
          to the part that came in, to everything, or to nothing. Because each reading gives
          a materially different answer, the calculator on this site refuses to compute a figure
          for this case rather than guess.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <Callout
        tone="caution"
        title="We are not going to guess at these for you"
        headingLevel={4}
      >
        <p>
          If your situation is one of the three above, nothing on this site — including
          the calculator — will give you a confident answer, because none exists yet in
          the law this project holds. Take it to the Inland Revenue Department or a
          qualified tax practitioner. Guidance from IRD, or the Y/A 2025/2026 return form
          once it is available, is the most likely thing to settle it.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>Do you still get personal relief against this income?</h2>
      <p>
        Very likely yes, but this is not stated in so many words anywhere this project
        holds. The 15% cap modifies the <em>rate</em> charged on the income; personal relief
        is deducted earlier, in arriving at taxable income, as a structural reading of the Fifth
        Schedule.{' '}
        <CitationRef src="ira-2017" sources={taxYear.sources} label="Reasoning from" />{
          ' '
        }
        If your only income is foreign-source, this materially changes what you owe, so treat
        it as probable rather than certain until it is confirmed against a primary source.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What this means if this is your situation</h2>
      <p>
        The remittance question is asked as a route picker in the calculator, not a yes/no
        — because "did you remit through a bank" is not a question most people can
        honestly answer. Work out your figure with your actual routes on the{' '}
        <a href="/calculator/">calculator</a>, which will show you a computed figure for
        the routes the law does settle, and refuse a figure — explaining why — for the
        ones it does not.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__footer">
      <p class="guide__footer-note">
        This is decision support, not tax advice. Verify with the Inland Revenue
        Department or a qualified tax practitioner before you rely on any figure here to
        file or pay.
      </p>
      <ul class="guide__footer-links">
        <li><a href="/calculator/">Work out your figure</a></li>
        <li><a href="/rates/">Rates for this year</a></li>
        <li><a href="/about/sources/">Sources cited on this page</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
